{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791367830,"nonce":"UIlA25xhRe0LfFMo4mu91JOJGm4=","title":"Nullable scalar comparisons lose null identity when operands are opened","message":"Enabling Rayzor's missing target.static alias activates TestOps.testNadakoOps. It produces 49 failed false assertions: nullable Int, Float and Bool operands are opened as primitive zero/false before comparison, so null compares equal to zero/false and takes ordinary numeric ordering. Both operands are lowered once; the null tags need to remain available while comparing their primitive payloads.\n\nThe upstream TestOps target.static branch requires null to equal only null and to be unordered against numeric values in either operand position. The installed native Haxe interpreter is an eval target; its nullable Int ordering sorts null before values, so those static-target ordering assertions are not a valid --interp cross-check. NullableUnaryArithmetic.hx guards that subset with target.static, as the upstream test does, while shared equality, unary, numeric and boxed-argument assertions pass native Haxe --interp.\n\nRegression coverage includes null/null equality and inequality, null/zero and null/false, both operand orders for relational operators under target.static, nullable Float values and mixed non-null nullable Int/Float comparison. Preserve boxed tags before opening numeric slots, then combine the scalar comparison with presence tests: equality includes both-null, inequality includes exactly-one-null, all ordering requires both values to be present. TestOps now passes all 279 assertions in /tmp/rayzor-nullable-unary-focused-v4/report.tsv after this and the related numeric-operator fixes; full validation is pending.","files":null}]}